# Secrets for AlertLoop. Copy to .env and set real values (.env is gitignored).
#
# This file holds SECRETS ONLY. Everything else — channels, routing, API keys,
# worker and rate-limit tuning — lives in the YAML config file. The variables
# below do nothing on their own: they are read only where the config file asks
# for them by name, written as ${VAR} or ${VAR:-default}. See
# alertloop.example.yaml and the Configuration section of README.md.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32       # run it twice: admin token, database password
#
# Compose reads .env from the project directory: the one holding
# docker-compose.yml, at the repo root. Both profiles live in that one file.

# Which deployment `docker compose up -d` starts:
#   demo      one all-in-one container on SQLite, loopback only, no setup
#   postgres  separate api and worker containers on PostgreSQL (production;
#             also needs alertloop.yaml — cp alertloop.example.yaml first)
#
# EDIT this line to switch, and do not pass --profile: for that one command the
# flag REPLACES this value, so it would act on a different deployment than
# every other command you run here.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=demo

# The port on THIS host where AlertLoop is published, on 127.0.0.1 only. 8080
# when unset; change it here if something else already holds 8080. Only the
# host side moves — inside the container AlertLoop still listens on 8080 — so
# point your reverse proxy and ALERTLOOP_URL in /etc/alertloop/monit.env at the
# new port. Read by Compose only; it is not passed to AlertLoop.
# ALERTLOOP_PORT=8080

# Pin the image in production. `latest` moves under you on the next
# `docker compose pull`, which is the last thing you want from an alerting
# service. Set it to a build of your own to run unreleased code.
# Image tags are the version without the leading "v" of the git tag:
# 0.5.2, not v0.5.2.
# ALERTLOOP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/golovanov-dev/alertloop:0.5.2

# Signs in to /admin and grants full API access. Sent on every request, so keep
# it off plaintext HTTP — reach the service through the HTTPS reverse proxy.
#
# LEFT EMPTY ON PURPOSE. alertloop.example.yaml references it with no fallback,
# so an empty value stops the postgres profile at startup and names what is
# missing — which is the point: a placeholder shipped in this repository would
# start, and would leave the whole API open to anyone who read it here.
# The demo profile has its own loopback-only fallback and still comes up.
# GENERATE A REAL ONE:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
ALERTLOOP_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# Used by the PostgreSQL Compose profile. The compose file builds the DSN from
# it, so it is set in exactly one place — nothing to keep in sync with the
# config file. Also empty on purpose: the postgres image refuses to initialise
# without a password, so the profile stops instead of creating a database on a
# published one.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# A hex password needs nothing more. One of your own must have no spaces and no
# backslashes and must not start with a single quote: the DSN the compose file
# builds reads those differently. Do not percent-encode it. In THIS file, a
# value containing "$" or "#" goes in single quotes — POSTGRES_PASSWORD='...' —
# because Compose expands "$" and can take "#" for a comment in an unquoted
# value; the quotes are not part of the password.
#
# The postgres image reads it only when it creates the database; changing it
# here later does not change the password inside an existing one (see
# "Changing the database password" in OPERATIONS.md).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Optional: also write the logs of the postgres profile to files on the host,
# next to stdout. Both are empty by default, which means stdout only.
#
# Before setting them, create the directory and give it to the container user:
#   mkdir -p logs && sudo chown 10001:10001 logs
# The containers run as uid 10001 and cannot write to a root-owned directory —
# they would stop at startup with "permission denied". Details, including how
# to read the logs without files, are in OPERATIONS.md.
# ALERTLOOP_LOG_FILE_API=/var/log/alertloop/api.log
# ALERTLOOP_LOG_FILE_WORKER=/var/log/alertloop/worker.log
